<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuringian Forest</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thuringian Forest is a wooded mountain range in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, running about 150 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> valley south-east towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-highlands">Thuringian Highlands</a>; its highest peak, the Großer Beerberg, reaches 983 metres. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period its slopes were divided among small territories such as the counties of Henneberg and Schwarzburg, which drew income from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, glassworks and mining.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest.jpg" alt="Thuringian Forest" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: holger mohaupt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vom_Schneekopf_nach_Oberhof_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Thuringian Forest runs north-west to south-east through the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> valley skirts it on the south-west, and beyond its north-eastern side lies the Thuringian Basin, which includes the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>. Eisenach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> stand among the foothills at its northern foot, and Ilmenau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhl">Suhl</a> sit in dips on the range itself. It is a continuous chain of ancient rounded mountains with steep slopes on both sides, and it can be crossed easily only through a few passes.</p><p>Geologically the range is an uplifted fault block of sandstones, conglomerates, granites, gneisses and volcanic rock. Its uplift, understood as a long-range effect of the Alpine orogeny, began in the upper Cretaceous and ended in the late Tertiary after about 40 million years. Humid air arrives mainly from the west, so the western slopes and the crest receive the most rain, while the Thuringian Basin on the leeward side is one of the driest regions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ore deposits associated with the uplift have been of historical importance to the region, notably in the metalworking tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhl">Suhl</a> and the mining history of Ilmenau. The name of the Großer Eisenberg bears witness to past ore mining. Mining for fluorite, baryte and limonite was tried several times in the Little Thuringian Forest, a narrow ridge south of Suhl, but was given up as unprofitable because of difficult drainage.</p><p>The Rennsteig, an ancient path, follows the main ridge and connects the summits. It marks the traditional boundary between the hilly terrain of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the more rugged terrain of southern Germany, and also the boundary between the cultural regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, with different dialects, customs and costumes on either side. The slopes were once divided among small states: at the Großer Dreiherrnstein the territories of Schwarzburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and Henneberg met at a tripoint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg Castle</a>, near Eisenach, is where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> lived in exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The forest is an important year-round tourist destination, with winter sports facilities of international significance around Oberhof. The Rennsteig is the most popular long-distance hiking trail in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and the Rennsteiglied, written about it, is the unofficial hymn of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Sights include the Marienglashöhle, a selenite cave in a former mine near Friedrichroda, the firearms museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhl">Suhl</a>, and the historic town centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> and Ilmenau.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thuringian Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Henneberg family appears in the late 11th century; its counts held the burgraviate of Würzburg and built Henneberg castle south of the <b>Thuringian Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-henneberg">County of Henneberg</a></li><li><blockquote>Sizzo III, who held lands in the <b>Thuringian Forest</b>, called himself count of Schwarzburg in 1123 after the castle on the Schwarza river.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-schwarzburg">County of Schwarzburg</a></li></ul></section>
